---
import Cite from '../../components/Cite.astro';
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
import { codexPage } from '../../data/pages.js';

const page = codexPage('circular-economies');
---
<Article slug={page.slug}>
  <p>
    In a <strong>circular economy</strong>, people are paid in bitcoin, spend it at local shops, and those shops pay their own staff and
    suppliers in bitcoin. The money circulates in the community instead of being converted back to dollars at every step.<Cite page={page} s="beachPaper" />
  </p>

  <h2>Bitcoin Beach</h2>
  <p>
    The best-known attempt began in 2019 in El Zonte, a small surf town on the Pacific coast of El Salvador. Funded by an anonymous
    donation, the Bitcoin Beach project paid for community work in bitcoin and helped local businesses accept it.<Cite page={page} s="beach" /><Cite page={page} s="beachWork" />
    Lightning wallets made the small, everyday payments of a town practical.
  </p>

  <h2>From a town to a country</h2>
  <p>
    In September 2021 El Salvador made bitcoin legal tender nationwide. The International Monetary Fund warned about the risks and urged
    the government to narrow the law.<Cite page={page} s="imf2021" /> In 2025, as part of a new IMF arrangement, accepting bitcoin
    became voluntary for businesses.<Cite page={page} s="imf2025" />
  </p>

  <h2>What it has to do with federations</h2>
  <p>
    A town that pays in bitcoin needs wallets that are simple, cheap and private. Community custody, where trusted local guardians run a
    mint, is one way to offer that. It is also why Byzantine Council is set in an island town: the federation you guard is meant to serve
    the people around it.
  </p>

  <h2>In the game</h2>
  <p>
    Beach Town, a hub island coming in a later update, will have shops and courier jobs paid in play sats. Play sats are points: they
    cannot be bought, sold or withdrawn.
  </p>
</Article>
